THIRD-PARTY CODE, COPIED AND MODIFIED. READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING ANYTHING
UNDER THIS DIRECTORY.

Upstream:   golang.org/x/crypto
Version:    v0.37.0
            (the exact version cbeuw/Cloak's own go.mod pins, which is the
            point -- the oracle must run the Salsa20 the reference
            implementation runs, not a nearby one)
Licence:    BSD-3-Clause, ./LICENSE, plus ./PATENTS. Both are upstream's
            own files, unmodified. Per-file copyright headers are intact
            and must stay intact.
Source:     https://cs.opensource.google/go/x/crypto/+/v0.37.0

Packages copied, in full apart from the edit below:

    ./salsa20          <- golang.org/x/crypto/salsa20
    ./salsa20/salsa    <- golang.org/x/crypto/salsa20/salsa
    ./alias            <- golang.org/x/crypto/internal/alias
                          (pulled in because salsa20.go imports it;
                          upstream's path has an extra `internal/` segment
                          that this module cannot spell)

THE ONE EDIT, and there is exactly one:

    import "golang.org/x/crypto/internal/alias"   -> "oracle/internal/alias"
    import "golang.org/x/crypto/salsa20/salsa"    -> "oracle/internal/salsa20/salsa"

both in ./salsa20/salsa20.go. Nothing else was changed: no logic, no
constants, no build tags, no file was renamed. `diff -r` against the
upstream tarball for v0.37.0 should show those two lines and the removals
noted below and nothing more.

WHAT WAS REMOVED, and why that is not an edit to the crypto:

    salsa20/salsa20_test.go        (139 lines)
    salsa20/salsa/salsa_test.go     (54 lines)

The CMake rule that builds this oracle only ever runs `go build`; `go test`
is never run against this directory by anything in this repository. Those
two files therefore ran nowhere, and a reader who found them would
reasonably mistake them for coverage this tree does not have. No non-test
file was touched.

WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY *NOT* REMOVED:

    salsa20/salsa/salsa20_amd64.s   (880 lines of assembly)
    salsa20/salsa/salsa20_amd64.go

This project's dev image is aarch64 (`go version` reports linux/arm64), so
salsa20_noasm.go is what compiles here and the amd64 assembly is dead ON
THIS MACHINE ONLY. It is live on any amd64 CI runner or contributor laptop.
Deleting it would make a vendored cryptographic package silently
architecture-dependent -- i.e. it would be a real modification to
third-party crypto, disguised as tidying -- which is a strictly worse
failure than carrying 880 lines nobody reads here.

WHY A COPY AT ALL, rather than a module dependency. The dev image's module
cache (GOMODCACHE=/go/pkg/mod) is populated by Dockerfile.dev with
github.com/gorilla/websocket and nothing else, and the image has no
network at build time, so `require golang.org/x/crypto v0.37.0` does not
resolve. Adding it to the `go mod download` in Dockerfile.dev would remove
this whole directory (~1,500 lines) for one line of Dockerfile, and that
is the right long-term shape; it is recorded here as the alternative a
reader should reach for rather than as a defence of the copy.

WHY A COPY RATHER THAN A REIMPLEMENTATION -- which is the load-bearing
reason and is not about convenience. This directory exists to be an
ORACLE: an implementation of Cloak's wire format that this project did not
write. The C side's Salsa20 has only round-trip tests of its own, so a
hand-written Go Salsa20 would have been a second unverified implementation
agreeing with the first, which is precisely the defect class the oracle was
built to catch. Upstream's own bytes are the property being bought here.

See ../cloak_upstream.go's edit list (E2) for how this package is reached
from the oracle's own code.
